{"qid":"Q241790","names":["Kingdom of Kush"],"title":"Kingdom of Kush","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Kush was an ancient Nile Valley state centered in Upper Nubia (modern Sudan) that emerged as an organized polity around 1000 BCE at Napata, near the Fourth Cataract, and endured until its conquest by the kingdom of Aksum c. 350 CE. At its zenith in the eighth century BCE the Kushite kings of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty conquered Egypt: Piye defeated the rival Delta rulers c. 728 BCE and his successor Taharqa (r. 690–664 BCE) presided over Egypt and Nubia simultaneously before Assyrian invasions under Esarhaddon and Ashurbanipal expelled the Kushites from the Nile Delta. The capital subsequently shifted to Meroë (c. 270 BCE), where the kingdom developed a distinct Meroitic script, conducted iron smelting on a large scale, and sustained pyramid-building traditions; Aksumite king Ezana destroyed Meroë around 350 CE, closing the Meroitic state.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core Kushite territory along the Nile from the Fourth Cataract to Meroë is well attested by temples, pyramids, and inscriptions; the lateral extent into the eastern desert, Kordofan, and sub-Saharan savannas is reconstructed from trade goods and tribute lists and remains approximate, particularly for earlier periods.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Kush","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kush"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Kush","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kush"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Kush","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Alara","nativeName":"Ala